# Security headers for every response Cloudflare serves.
#
# `img-src https:` is deliberate and is the one loosening here: the BIMI test
# renders the logo the domain under test publishes, which is by definition on
# somebody else's host. Images cannot execute, and `script-src` stays locked to
# self.
#
# The whole product renders DNS records published by other people, so the
# script boundary matters more here than on a normal marketing site. The CSP
# below allows exactly what the site uses and nothing else.
#
# Astro emits its own `_headers` entries during build; this file is merged with
# them, so the `/_astro/*` cache rule lives there and the policy lives here.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflare-dns.com; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

# Reports are read in the browser. Nothing about this route may be cached by a
# shared cache, because the page holds somebody's mail data once it is used.
/bloodwork
  Cache-Control: private, no-store

# Nothing for /api/* here on purpose. This file decorates static asset
# responses, and every API route is built by the Worker instead, so a rule
# written here would look right and do nothing. Those headers are set in
# `src/lib/api.ts`, which is the code that actually builds the response.
